cans, or signs that Biggs is consolidating GOP-leaning independents while Hobbs' polling lead softens, could narrow the race in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against Republican Andy Biggs, a former congressman with a hard-right record, in a state that leans slightly Republican on paper. Polling through late August consistently shows Hobbs ahead, with an average margin of about eight points across roughly a dozen surveys, including several from Republican-leaning firms. Independent voters and women have broken her way in recent polls, and she has leaned into a GOP-friendly message on the campaign trail.\n\nHobbs holds a large financial edge, having raised roughly $10 million to Biggs' $3 million, and prediction markets price her chances near 89 percent. Most race raters call the contest Lean Democratic, with one at Likely Democratic. Biggs' challenge is winning over moderate and independent voters who have been skeptical of him; Hobbs' challenge is converting a polling lead into turnout in a state where recent statewide margins have often been razor-thin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9047, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9201, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9198, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8839, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8882, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8963, "margin": 7.0}]}